## Part 3 — permission posture: `Bash(*)` narrowed

`.claude/settings.json` previously allowed `Bash(*)` — every shell command,
pre-approved, for anyone who clones this repo — with only `Bash(rm -rf *)` and
`Bash(rm -r *)` denied. That pre-approved `curl`, `wget`, arbitrary interpreters,
`git push`, and `rm` in every form the two deny rules miss (`rm -f`, `rm -fr`,
`rm --recursive`).

Replaced with an allowlist of what this project actually runs, derived from
observed usage:

```
Bash(ls:*)  Bash(cat:*)  Bash(cd:*)
Bash(git status:*)  Bash(git diff:*)  Bash(git log:*)  Bash(git show:*)
Bash(pytest:*)  Bash(ruff check:*)
Bash(python3 tools/:*)  Bash(python tools/:*)
Bash(.venv/Scripts/python.exe tools/:*)
```

and a deny list extended to cover the `rm` variants, force-pushes, and
`curl`/`wget`.

**Two honest caveats:**

1. These are **prefix string matches, not a sandbox.** `Bash(python3 tools/:*)`
   pre-approves anything beginning with that string, path traversal included. It
   is a large improvement over `Bash(*)`, not a security boundary.
2. Anything not on the allowlist is **not blocked** — it falls through to the
   permission system as normal. With auto mode as the default, routine commands
   are still approved by the safety classifier without prompting, so the
   practical cost of the tighter list is close to zero.

